  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 248 AM CDT Mon Jul 20 2026\n\nAs of 230 AM Monday morning, longwave, midlevel ridging continues to \nreside across the western 2/3rds of the CONUS with troughing off the \nwest coast and across the northeast US. A shortwave trough was \nadvancing along the ND/Canadian border with an attendant surface \ntrough axis across northern MN through SD. Midlevel heights will \nincrease today across from the central Rockies through the central \nPlains as the ridge axis amplifies. This will yield slightly warmer \ntemperatures when compared to Sunday as 850 mb temperatures range \nfrom 21-23 C across southeast KS to 26-28 C across central KS. This \nwill translate to afternoon high temperatures ranging from the mid \nto upper 90s across southeast KS to near 100 across central KS. \nFactoring dew points in the 60s to near 70 will create heat index \nvalues near 105 areawide. A Heat Advisory remains in effect from 1-9 \nPM today for the entire forecast area.\n\nA second shortwave trough, currently entering northwest MT, is \nprogged to dive southeast from the northern Plains into the Great \nLakes region late today into Tuesday, shunting a weak cold front \ninto the area Tuesday. Model guidance remains consistent with the \nfrontal zone nearing the Highway-400 corridor by midday Tuesday. \nThis will set the stage for another afternoon of extreme heat across \nsouthern KS with temperatures near 100 once again. Factoring in dew \npoints near 70 will create heat index values up to 110. The Extreme \nWatch remains in effect from noon to 9 PM Tuesday for areas from \nReno to Allen counties and points south. Areas within the post-\nfrontal airmass will remain quite warm with high temperatures in the \nmid-90s. Dew points behind the front will decrease into 50s and 60s, \nmitigating the potential for extreme heat. \n\nMid range guidance continues to suggest central, south-central, and \nsoutheast KS will remain on the northeastern periphery of the \nmidlevel ridge axis through Wednesday. A shortwave trough is progged \nto eject from the Intermountain West region into high Plains late \nWednesday night into Thursday morning. This will result in the \ndevelopment of a surface trough across the high Plains. A \nstrengthening LLJ Thursday morning may yield a few thunderstorms \nacross portions of northern KS. A more-favorable thunderstorm \npattern appears to emerge late Thursday into Friday morning as the \naforementioned shortwave trough ejects into the Plains with a 45-50 \nkt LLJ developing across central KS. At this point the nose of the \nLLJ looks to setup across northern KS and may keep the best rain \nchances from northern into northeast KS. Additional storms are \npossible Friday night into Saturday morning as the surface trough \nslides across southeast KS. The core of the midlevel ridge appears \nto slide back over the central Plains by next weekend. This will \nlikely yield warmer temperatures and minimal rain chances from \nSaturday afternoon into next week.",
